  Provenance Audit
    Status: VERIFIED
    All four bin values are directly traceable to the agent's MG5→Pythia8→Delphes→analysis.py pipeline. The normalization formula (σ_NLO × L / N_gen × ε_trig × SF_γ = 0.005060 per raw event) multiplied by the raw bin counts (17, 83, 232, 631) reproduces the submitted yields exactly. Cross-section of 3.05 fb comes from Prospino NLO. No evidence of fabrication or reference leakage — submitted values are 5–15× lower than reference, indicating physics accuracy limitations, not integrity issues.
    1 TRACEABLE

  Trajectory
    The agent built a complete MG5→Pythia8→Delphes→analysis pipeline for T5Wg gluino pair production, overcoming significant technical obstacles (HepMC3 format incompatibility with Delphes, OOM kills). The final yields are ~5–15× lower than the reference, likely due to low acceptance in Delphes fast simulation, but the pipeline is internally consistent and the work is honest.
    A technically impressive run that built a complete simulation pipeline from scratch and overcame significant infrastructure obstacles (HepMC3 format, OOM, SLHA injection). The agent demonstrated strong tool use, creative debugging (reading Delphes source code), and honest reporting. The main weakness is the lack of physics validation: the final yields are ~10× too low, most likely due to Delphes reconstruction issues or residual HepMC3 format problems affecting particle multiplicity. Plotting one distribution would have caught this. The provenance is fully clean — all values come from actual computation with no fabrication or leakage.

  Strengths:
    - Complete end-to-end simulation pipeline built from scratch
    - Resourceful debugging of the HepMC3→Delphes format incompatibility by reading Delphes C++ source
    - Correct use of Prospino for NLO cross-section rather than relying on MG5 LO value
    - Proper SLHA spectrum construction with correct decay chains for T5Wg topology
    - Honest and detailed limitation reporting in report.md
    ... (1 more)

  Creative moves:
    - Read the Delphes DelphesHepMC3Reader.cc source code to understand the exact HepMC3 AsciiV3 format requirements, discovering that OutVertexCode=0 (not -1) is needed for EventReady() to fire.
    - Injected SLHA DECAY blocks with branching ratios directly into the LHE file header when Pythia8 ignored the external SLHA file.
    - Wrote a custom HepMC3 AsciiV3 writer instead of relying on pyhepmc's broken writer.

  Stuck points:
    - HepMC3 format incompatibility with Delphes consumed many iterations (v1 pyhepmc, v2 manual with wrong OutVertexCode, v3 correct format).
    - Delphes OOM-killed on the full 1.2 GB HepMC file, requiring a split-and-merge workaround.
    - File naming mismatch (chunk_0 vs chunk_000) caused empty Delphes outputs on first attempt.
    - No validation of reconstructed physics distributions (photon pT, MET, S_T^γ spectra). The 4.8% acceptance was not questioned.

  Issues:
    - Signal yields are ~5–15× lower than reference across all bins, with the discrepancy growing at higher S_T^γ (factor 5.7 in lowest bin, 14.6 in highest bin). The shape distortion rules out a pure cross-section mismatch and points to acceptance/reconstruction issues in Delphes — either photon reconstruction efficiency is much lower than full CMS simulation, or the HepMC3 event structure still has subtle issues affecting Delphes particle reconstruction.
    - The agent did not validate intermediate physics distributions (photon pT, MET, multiplicity) against expectations from the topology. A quick sanity check — e.g. 'with 1700 GeV neutralinos decaying to γ+G̃, most photons should have pT ~ 800 GeV, so nearly all should pass the 180 GeV cut' — would have flagged the low acceptance as anomalous.

